+\emph{2) Reassembly Weight Generation:}
+Pixel Shuffle rearranges the predicted weights to match the spatial resolution of the upsampled feature map. With $s=2$, the 100 values predicted at each input position are distributed among its four corresponding output positions, giving 25 weights to each. For each output position $(i,j)$, Softmax normalizes the corresponding $k^2$ weights:
+\begin{equation}
+K_{i,j}=\operatorname{softmax}\!\left(
+[\operatorname{PS}(K')]_{:,i,j}\right).
+\label{eq:cafr-kernel}
+\end{equation}
+With $k=5$, these 25 normalized weights determine the contributions of the positions within a $5\times5$ input neighborhood.
+
+\emph{3) Feature Reassembly:}
+In parallel with weight prediction, Unfold extracts a $k\times k$ neighborhood around each input position. During $2\times$ upsampling, each input position corresponds to four output positions that share the same input neighborhood but have their own predicted weights. The output feature at position $(i,j)$ is computed as
+\begin{equation}
+Y_{c,i,j}=\sum_{u=1}^{k^2}K_{i,j,u}
+P_{c,\lfloor i/s\rfloor,\lfloor j/s\rfloor,u}.
+\label{eq:cafr-reassembly}
+\end{equation}
+Here, $P$ denotes the extracted input neighborhoods, $u$ indexes the $k^2$ positions within each neighborhood, and $c$ denotes the feature channel. The floor indices map an output position to its corresponding source position. The predicted weights are shared across feature channels, producing $Y\in\mathbb{R}^{C\times sH\times sW}$. Unlike nearest-neighbor interpolation, CAFR aggregates the input neighborhood using a separate set of weights for each output position, allowing the four positions to produce different feature responses.
